{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Chance-Constrained Belief-State Retrosynthesis Planning for Mechanism-Aware AI Chemistry",
      "motivation": "AI will redefine the future of chemistry only if it moves beyond isolated reaction predictors toward trustworthy multi-step synthesis decision-making under scarce, noisy, and biased experimental data. Graph-to-sequence models have shown that retrosynthesis and reaction prediction can be learned in an end-to-end manner [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3206796875), and mechanism-driven graph learning has begun to connect retrosynthetic prediction with interpretable pathway planning [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308632600). Chemistry-aware bond-change models improve data efficiency and reaction-outcome plausibility [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4392515737), while meta-learning enables selectivity prediction in low-data asymmetric catalysis [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4409453348). Despite this progress, a central bottleneck remains: current systems usually predict single-step outcomes but do not propagate calibrated uncertainty through an entire synthetic route, so a route can look promising under point estimates yet fail because of accumulated feasibility, yield, or selectivity risk. This gap is not solved by simply adding a confidence score; it requires a planning mechanism that treats synthesis as a sequential decision problem under numeric uncertainty. Operations research provides belief-state and contingent planning frameworks for acting under incomplete information [Author et al., 2006](https://openalex.org/W6995894472), heuristic forward-chaining search with chance constraints for numeric uncertainty [Author et al., 2016](https://openalex.org/W2531061645), and efficient horizon-wise uncertainty propagation from stochastic model predictive control [Author et al., 2017](https://openalex.org/W2800906245). The primary testable hypothesis here is that, with the reaction predictor and mechanism constraints held fixed, chance-constrained belief-state route planning causally increases the fraction of proposed multi-step syntheses that satisfy feasibility and selectivity constraints compared with point-estimate planning at equal compute.",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the problem of proposing reliable multi-step organic syntheses when reaction feasibility, yield, and selectivity predictions are uncertain; it can be divided into 4 parts: a frozen mechanism-aware reaction belief module, a stochastic route-transition and chance-constraint module, a belief-state forward-chaining planner, and a controlled evaluation and replanning protocol.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Frozen mechanism-aware reaction belief module",
          "description": "This component is deliberately held fixed in the primary test so that planner behavior, not predictor quality, is the causal variable. It uses a mechanism-driven graph retrosynthesis model in the spirit of MechRetro to propose chemically plausible disconnections and to encode reaction centers, bond changes, and mechanism-level constraints [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308632600). It can be initialized from template-free graph-to-sequence retrosynthesis models that learn transformations without hand-written rules [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3206796875). For outcome prediction, it uses chemistry-aware bond-change representations related to NERF to improve data efficiency and chemical plausibility [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4392515737), and it can include a meta-learning head for enantioselectivity in low-data catalytic tasks [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4409453348). The module outputs, for each candidate step, a calibrated probability of feasibility and distributional estimates for yield and selectivity, together with a hard mechanism-constraint flag."
        },
        {
          "name": "Stochastic route-transition and chance-constraint module",
          "description": "This component transfers stochastic model predictive control's horizon-wise uncertainty propagation to multi-step synthesis planning [Author et al., 2017](https://openalex.org/W2800906245). Each retrosynthetic action is treated as a stochastic transition whose latent outcomes include feasibility, yield, and selectivity. The route success event is the conjunction of step-level feasibility and constraint satisfaction, such as minimum yield or required enantiomeric excess. The planner computes or approximates the probability of this conjunction by moment propagation, linearization, or Monte Carlo sampling. A chance constraint requires the route success probability to exceed a preset threshold, so routes with high expected yield but catastrophic tail risk are rejected."
        },
        {
          "name": "Belief-state forward-chaining route planner",
          "description": "This component adapts belief-state planning under uncertainty from operations research [Author et al., 2006](https://openalex.org/W6995894472) and heuristic forward-chaining planning with numeric uncertainty [Author et al., 2016](https://openalex.org/W2531061645). The planner maintains a belief over which intermediates are available and what their likely yields, selectivities, and purification burdens are. It expands candidate disconnections forward from target to precursors, uses a heuristic that combines expected cost, step count, and risk penalty, and prunes branches whose upper-bound success probability cannot meet the chance constraint. The output is an interpretable contingent route with flags identifying the steps whose uncertainty most strongly threatens route success."
        },
        {
          "name": "Controlled evaluation and optional replanning interface",
          "description": "This component provides a rigorous evaluation layer and a later-stage replanning interface. In the first phase, replanning is disabled so that the effect of chance-constrained uncertainty propagation can be isolated from the effect of adaptive experimentation. In later phases, high-uncertainty steps trigger suggested validation experiments or updated literature evidence, and the belief state is updated before replanning, following contingent planning principles [Author et al., 2006](https://openalex.org/W6995894472). The component also records mechanism-level explanations, such as disallowed bond changes or low-confidence selectivity predictions, so that chemists can audit why a route was accepted or rejected [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308632600)."
        }
      ],
