    {
      "title": "Mechanism-Conditioned Belief-State Synthesis Planning with Correlated Incompatibility Variables",
      "motivation": "AI will redefine the future of chemistry only when it can propose multi-step syntheses that chemists can trust under scarce, noisy, and biased experimental data. Current models have improved isolated tasks, including template-free retrosynthesis and reaction prediction [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3206796875), mechanism-driven interpretable retrosynthesis [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308632600), chemistry-aware bond-change outcome prediction [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4392515737), and few-shot selectivity prediction for asymmetric catalysis [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4409453348). However, these approaches still mostly score single steps or aggregate route scores through a generic independent-step conjunction of per-step probabilities. Real routes fail through latent shared mechanistic incompatibilities, protecting-group/chemoselectivity states, catalyst poisoning, stereochemical erosion, and correlated side-reaction risks that propagate across steps. A route can have high per-step scores but fail because a hidden condition created in one step invalidates a later step. This idea therefore transfers belief-state planning under uncertainty from probabilistic planning [Author et al., 2017](https://openalex.org/W7048571973) and continuous-to-symbolic uncertainty bridging from neuro-symbolic reasoning [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W7106207643) to create a synthesis planner that represents, propagates, and reduces mechanism-level route uncertainty rather than only improving single-step predictors.",
