      "title": "Causal Mechanism-State Model Predictive Planning for Repairable Retrosynthesis",
      "motivation": "AI can redefine chemistry only if planning systems move from isolated step prediction to robust, repairable multi-step synthesis. Template-free graph-to-sequence models showed that retrosynthesis and reaction prediction can be learned end-to-end [Author et al., 2021](https://openalex.org/W3206796875). Mechanism-aware bond-change models improve data efficiency and chemical plausibility for reaction outcomes [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4392515737), and meta-learning addresses scarce selectivity data in asymmetric catalysis [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4409453348). MechRetro further demonstrates that chemical mechanism can make retrosynthesis and pathway planning more interpretable [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4308632600). However, current planners still lack a causal intervention semantics for route repair: they do not predict how changing a protecting group, catalyst, purification, or workup will alter correlated mechanistic incompatibilities, nor do they plan under calibrated uncertainty. Predictive-control research provides the needed mechanism: learning-based model predictive control enables safe exploration with learned dynamics and explicit constraints [Author et al., 2019](https://openalex.org/W2791704483), while the state-space versus direct multi-step predictor comparison motivates a controlled test of recursive mechanistic state models for sequential chemistry decisions [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4224330578).",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve uncertainty-brittle multi-step synthesis planning by making route repair an explicit causal control problem; it can be divided into 4 parts: a causal mechanism-state representation, a learnable incompatibility-graph learner, an intervention-conditioned transition model, and a receding-horizon constrained repair planner. The primary hypothesis is that, with frozen step predictors and matched model capacity, a recursive mechanism-state model with intervention variables predicts counterfactual route repairs and achieves higher validated route success than a direct multi-step route predictor or independent step aggregation. The mechanism is tested first by varying only the state-transition structure while holding data, predictors, constraints, and compute fixed.",
